diff --git a/src/engine/query/parse.rs b/src/engine/query/parse.rs
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d0a264a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/engine/query/parse.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,314 @@
+//! The grammar: what a query text means, and what it refuses.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.11-query-engine`. The text a caller types is *restricted SQL*, and the word
+//! "restricted" is the whole design: a store whose answers are computed from closed segments and a
+//! manifest needs a query it can plan, and every clause that would make planning impossible is
+//! left out rather than implemented badly. This module is the shape of what is left:
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! SELECT <agg>(value) FROM <metric> WHERE <pred> [AND <pred>]*
+//!       GROUP BY time(<window>)[, series] [LIMIT <n>]
+//! <pred>   ::= <key> = '<value>' | ts BETWEEN <millis> AND <millis>
+//! <agg>    ::= count | sum | mean | min | max      (case-insensitive, like every keyword)
+//! <window> ::= <n><unit>, <unit> in ms | s | m | h | d
+//! ```
+//!
+//! # The six decisions, and what each one buys
+//!
+//! * **the time range is mandatory, and half-open.** `ts BETWEEN a AND b` means `[a, b)`, and a
+//!   query without it is refused. A store's ranges are half-open everywhere else — `Range`,
+//!   `Partition`, the manifest's per-segment span (`lakelet/4.9-retention`) — and an inclusive
+//!   upper end would make two answers that meet at a millisecond both claim the record on it. The
+//!   *mandatory* half is the more interesting one: a telemetry query that scans all of time is not
+//!   a question, it is an accident, and the accident is answered with a sentence instead of with a
+//!   fleet's worth of records.
+//! * **`FROM` names the metric, not the series.** A record's series is `metric,tag=value,…`
+//!   (`lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s exact-match rule, and [`Series`] reads it), so `FROM cpu` is
+//!   every series whose metric is `cpu` and each `WHERE <key> = '<value>'` keeps only the records
+//!   whose series carries that pair. `ts` is not a tag: it is the time axis, and a predicate on it
+//!   is refused in a sentence that says where the range is written instead.
+//! * **`AND` only.** Every predicate has to hold, and an `OR` is refused rather than implemented.
+//!   This is the one clause whose absence changes what a *plan* may do — a record that satisfies
+//!   either half cannot be ruled out by a predicate that matches one half — and
+//!   `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning` is where the argument is made in full. Declining it now is what
+//!   keeps this step's plan honest.
+//! * **`GROUP BY series` is what makes an answer per series.** Without it, one answer mixes every
+//!   matching series — `lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s `None`, which is the right answer for "how
+//!   busy is the fleet" and the wrong one for "how busy is this host", and the grammar makes the
+//!   caller say which they meant. With it, the answer is one bucket list per matching series,
+//!   ordered by series name.
+//! * **`LIMIT` bounds the number of series, so it needs `GROUP BY series`.** A limit on a single
+//!   answer names nothing to bound: the answer is one bucket list however many records went into
+//!   it, so `LIMIT 10` there would either do nothing or lie. Where it applies it is a plan's
+//!   cheapest friend — an operator asking for "the ten noisiest hosts" does not want the four
+//!   hundredth — and a limit of zero is refused for the same reason an empty range is.
+//! * **the window is mandatory, and it is a count of the absolute grid.** `GROUP BY
+//!   time(<n><unit>)` is how a caller says how wide one bucket is, and there is no default: a
+//!   query that does not say has not said what it wants, and every answer this grammar gives is a
+//!   list of buckets whose edges are on the grid `lakelet/4.10-downsampling` fixed. The units are
+//!   that step's `--window` table and [`unit_millis`] is the one copy of it — it is *public* and
+//!   *shared*, because two copies of "what is `5m`" are two answers to `--window 90x`.
+//!
+//! # What this module does not decide
+//!
+//! The grammar is the whole of the *shape* of a query. Three things are deliberately elsewhere:
+//!
+//! * **how many buckets the question asks for** — the range divided by the window — is arithmetic
+//!   the command line does before it opens a store, against the `MAX_WINDOWS`
+//!   `lakelet/4.10-downsampling` established ([`Plan::windows_of`] is the one copy of it, so both
+//!   input forms refuse the same typo with the same number);
